# Memor — Project Memory (MANDATORY)

This project uses [memor](https://github.com/akashchekka/memor) for persistent AI memory.
You MUST follow these rules. Skipping any step is a violation.

## Prefer the MCP tools
When tools named `memory_context`, `memory_add`, `memory_search`, `code_get` and `code_save` are available, use them. Fall back to the `memor` CLI only when they are not. Never mix both in one turn.

| Purpose | Tool | CLI fallback |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Load prior context | `memory_context` | `memor context` |
| Record a memory | `memory_add` | `memor add` |
| Search memories | `memory_search` | `memor search` |
| Check a stored file summary | `code_get` | `memor code load <file>` |
| Store a file summary | `code_save` | `memor code save <file>` |

## RULE 1: Load context once per conversation
Call `memory_context` on the first user message, before any other tool. Do not call it again on later turns.

## RULE 2: Check before reading a source file
Call `code_get` before opening any source file. This is the only mid-turn call.
- `status: fresh` — trust the stored summary and do NOT read the file.
- `status: stale`, `missing` or `unrecorded` — read the file, then `code_save` at end of turn.

Follow the `recommendation` field it returns.

## RULE 3: Write at the end of the turn
After producing your response, as the final tool calls of the turn:
- `memory_add` — 2-3 self-contained sentences capturing the decision, the reasoning, and what was rejected. Set `type` to `semantic` (decisions, architecture), `episodic` (bugs fixed, events), `procedural` (commands, workflows), or `preference` (style conventions). Defaults to `semantic`.
- `code_save` — for each source file you read or changed this turn.

Tag every entry, reusing existing tags. Consistent tags are what make retrieval work.

## RULE 4: Record only what is worth remembering
Memory is token-budgeted, so every low-value entry crowds out a useful one.

Record: decisions and why, rejected alternatives, non-obvious constraints, conventions, reproducible workflows, root causes.

Skip: restatements of the code, anything already stored, routine edits, and facts recoverable by reading the file.

## RULE 5: NEVER edit .memor/ files
Use the tools, or the CLI when tools are unavailable. NEVER use file-editing tools on anything under `.memor/`.